+## What changed since the completed upstream catch-up
+
+The original plan measured upstream **74afd55753ee7e6b34c57e3dc2e1a683b4c5a23b**, but the completed catch-up actually merged **a40d63a199f2d483fd27c9ad439f0d5efbc195aa** (merge **50786828f2186078f174fa020ad2d7f7460e258e**). The changes below therefore compare that **actual upstream baseline a40 → canonical viewer 4ad8986099314b9587df423c802db103a21d665d**, rather than comparing two private fork tips. The retained C5g target/source record and C7 merge-base/history evidence agree: this interval contains 82 upstream commits (51 nonmerges) and 794 changed paths. These are changes already present in the pinned upstream viewer; bringing their behavior into the private Rust/native branches is still planned work.
+
+1. **More flexible annotations and more faithful drawings.** A mark can change to a compatible kind in place—for example, highlight to underline—without losing its identity. Shapes, lines and ink can have no stroke while retaining their line-width geometry. Dash patterns now distinguish clearing a pattern from an invalid empty write, and imported solid/dashed borders read consistently. Ink follows fitted curves instead of straight segments between every recorded point, so its display, bounds and hit testing better match the saved PDF appearance.
+2. **Text edits and comments retain the intended formatting.** Formatting at a caret applies to the text typed next; formatting a selected text box applies across its text, with mixed selections reported correctly. Markup comments can read and write rich text alongside their plain-text projection. Font selection offers faces that can actually be written, missing Latin characters receive the standard-font fallback, and a delayed font answer can no longer redraw newly restyled text in its previous font. API consumers must carry the expanded rich-text fields and the text editor's returned paragraphs.
+3. **A page or part of a page can become a picture.** The snapshot tool lets someone drag a region, or click for the whole page, then copy the image or save a PNG from the viewer. Render calls gain crop-rectangle and rotation options, and snapshots respect rendering/copy permissions and pixel budgets. Integrations gain snapshot settings/events and image-copy/save command ports; image ownership, cancellation and browser clipboard activation remain acceptance requirements in the planned port.
+4. **Reading and navigating feel more predictable.** Page arrows and page-number controls step through a visible spread together; paged mode can turn on a wheel, swipe or flick past the page edge, with a setting to disable those gestures. A page-by-page choice is available in the View menu. Opening panels no longer scrolls the surrounding page; the text strip sits below its selection, annotation flags leave the style panel, the loading spinner stays in place, and the reader example keeps its bar visible. Short `/XYZ` link/bookmark destinations retain the coordinates they contain, while zoom zero means “keep the current zoom.” Stage callers gain current-page-group and turn-gesture APIs.
+5. **Cloud integrations and delivery tools advance too.** CloudPDF can enable modules per tenant, describe their permissions and usage meters, and report daily usage; its SDK/contracts and database migrations carry those changes. Licensing checks and idle PostgreSQL error handling are tightened, with a real module test host and more reliable SDK fakes. Existing 2.x documentation URLs redirect onward while linked sample files remain available, API snapshot generation works on Windows, and package prerelease history advances. These changes arrive through the TypeScript merge and their relevant checks; they do not imply a new Rust server project, release execution or notification activation.
+
+**Runtime is a separate choice.** The viewer above selects runtime **A: f1ba46f0dc3dc5f9b055a7311dda0c13ecc0f185**. Compared with the retained fork's upstream runtime baseline, A supplies the native annotation-kind/no-stroke, ink, rich-comment and font-rendering support, plus linearized-file layer and image/color handling fixes and dependency updates. It adds two C functions, so matching headers, generated bindings and built libraries must travel together; it does not replace the private fork's retained guarantees automatically. Optional runtime **B: eeac787c02fac7b40c74c91bbff0ffb5f59ff97e** is beyond that viewer gitlink: two history commits, one functional correction (**8bdd5c097be2c29f72a70dd13e0a1e98ce28f65c**) so rich comments use the reader's text defaults rather than drawing defaults. B remains a recommendation requiring an explicit implementation choice, not an already approved pin change.
+
